CH = dict(num=11, tag="Physics", title="Electricity", theme="Current, potential difference, Ohm's law, resistance, series and parallel, heating effect, power",
intro="Lamps, fans, heaters and mobile chargers all run on the flow of electric charge. Understanding current, voltage and resistance lets us design circuits, choose the right wire and fuse, and calculate the electricity bill. This chapter builds the basic quantities, states Ohm's law, compares series and parallel circuits and uses the heating effect of current.",
big_questions=["What is electric current and what drives it?", "How do resistances combine in series and parallel?", "How much heat and energy does a current produce?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "11.1 Electric Current and Potential Difference"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Electric current** is the rate of flow of charge: **I = Q/t**. One **ampere** is one coulomb of charge flowing per second. The charge on one electron is 1.6 x 10<sup>&minus;19</sup> C, so 1 C is about 6 x 10<sup>18</sup> electrons. Conventional current flows from the positive terminal to the negative, opposite to the flow of electrons."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Potential difference** between two points is the work done to move one coulomb of charge between them: **V = W/Q**. One **volt** is one joule per coulomb. A **cell** or battery maintains the potential difference; it is measured by a **voltmeter** connected in **parallel**, while current is measured by an **ammeter** connected in **series**."),
("table", ["Component", "Symbol idea", "Role"], [["Cell / battery", "Long line (+), short line (&minus;)", "Source of potential difference"], ["Plug key (switch)", "Open or closed", "Completes or breaks the circuit"], ["Ammeter", "A in a circle", "Measures current; low resistance; in series"], ["Voltmeter", "V in a circle", "Measures potential difference; high resistance; in parallel"], ["Resistor", "Zig-zag or box", "Opposes current"], ["Rheostat", "Variable resistor", "Changes current"]]),
("h2", "11.2 Ohm's Law and Resistance"),
("note", "FORMULA", "**Ohm's law:** at constant temperature, **V &prop; I**, i.e. **V = IR**. **R = V/I**; the SI unit of resistance is the **ohm (&Omega;)**; 1 &Omega; is the resistance when 1 V gives 1 A. The V&ndash;I graph of an ohmic conductor is a straight line through the origin; its slope is 1/R."),
("p", "Resistance arises because moving electrons collide with the atoms of the conductor. It depends on four things."),
("note", "FORMULA", "**R = &rho;L/A**, where &rho; is **resistivity** (unit &Omega; m). Resistance **increases** with length and with temperature (for metals) and **decreases** with cross-section area."),
("table", ["Type", "Resistivity range (&Omega; m)", "Examples"], [["Conductors", "10<sup>&minus;8</sup> to 10<sup>&minus;6</sup>", "Silver, copper, aluminium"], ["Alloys", "Higher than their metals, hardly oxidise when hot", "Nichrome, manganin, constantan"], ["Insulators", "10<sup>12</sup> to 10<sup>17</sup>", "Rubber, glass, ebonite"]]),
("ul", ["Alloys such as **nichrome** are used in heating elements because of high resistivity and no oxidation at high temperature.", "Copper and aluminium are used for **transmission lines** because of low resistivity.", "Tungsten has a high melting point (3380 &deg;C), so it is used for **bulb filaments**."]),
("h2", "11.3 Combination of Resistors"),
("table", ["Feature", "Series", "Parallel"], [["Current", "Same through each", "Divides among branches"], ["Potential difference", "Divides across resistors", "Same across each"], ["Equivalent resistance", "**R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> + R<sub>3</sub>**", "**1/R<sub>p</sub> = 1/R<sub>1</sub> + 1/R<sub>2</sub> + 1/R<sub>3</sub>**"], ["Value", "Greater than the largest", "Less than the smallest"], ["Failure", "One break stops all", "Others continue"], ["Use", "Fuse with appliance", "Home wiring"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "Domestic circuits use **parallel** connection so each appliance gets the full 220 V, works independently and draws its own current; in series a break in one appliance would stop all."),
("h2", "11.4 Heating Effect and Electric Power"),
("note", "FORMULA", "**Joule's law:** H = I<sup>2</sup>Rt (joules).  **Power:** P = VI = I<sup>2</sup>R = V<sup>2</sup>/R, unit **watt (W)**.  **Energy:** E = P x t; **1 kWh = 3.6 x 10<sup>6</sup> J**, the commercial unit (one unit of electricity)."),
("ul", ["Heating effect powers the electric heater, iron, toaster, geyser and the bulb filament.", "A **fuse** is a wire of low melting point and low rating in series; it melts and breaks the circuit during overloading or short-circuit and protects appliances.", "The same **current** flows in series, so heat is greatest in the **largest** resistor (H &prop; R); in parallel, with the same voltage, heat is greatest in the **smallest** resistor (H = V<sup>2</sup>t/R)."]),
("h2", "11.5 Worked Examples"),
("p", "**Ohm's law.** A resistor of 20 &Omega; across a 12 V battery: I = 12/20 = **0.6 A**; power = VI = 12 x 0.6 = **7.2 W**."),
("p", "**Series.** 2 &Omega;, 3 &Omega; and 5 &Omega; in series: R = **10 &Omega;**; with a 20 V supply I = 2 A and V across the 3 &Omega; resistor is 6 V."),
("p", "**Parallel.** 6 &Omega; and 3 &Omega;: 1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2, **R = 2 &Omega;**; with a 12 V supply the branch currents are 2 A and 4 A, total **6 A**."),
("p", "**Resistivity.** A wire of resistivity 1.6 x 10<sup>&minus;8</sup> &Omega; m, length 10 m and area 1 x 10<sup>&minus;6</sup> m<sup>2</sup> has R = 1.6 x 10<sup>&minus;8</sup> x 10 / 10<sup>&minus;6</sup> = **0.16 &Omega;**. If the wire is stretched to double its length, volume is constant so the area halves and **R becomes four times**."),
("p", "**Energy bill.** A 1000 W heater used 2 h a day for 30 days: energy = 1 x 2 x 30 = 60 kWh; at a rate of &rupee;6 per unit, cost = **&rupee;360**."),
("p", "**Heat produced.** A 100 &Omega; resistor carrying 2 A for 5 minutes: H = I<sup>2</sup>Rt = 4 x 100 x 300 = **1.2 x 10<sup>5</sup> J**."),
("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
("qa", [("Define potential difference.", "Work done per unit charge in moving a charge between two points: V = W/Q."), ("Why is the voltmeter connected in parallel?", "It must read the potential difference across a component and have very high resistance."), ("Why is tungsten used for filaments?", "High melting point and high resistivity."), ("State Joule's law.", "H = I<sup>2</sup>Rt."), ("How many joules in 1 kWh?", "3.6 x 10<sup>6</sup> J."), ("Why do we connect household appliances in parallel?", "Equal voltage, independent working."), ("What is the function of a fuse?", "It melts and breaks the circuit when current exceeds a safe value."), ("What happens to resistance if length is halved?", "It becomes half (R &prop; L).")]),
],
takeaways=["**I = Q/t; V = W/Q; V = IR.**", "**R = &rho;L/A**; alloys have higher resistivity than pure metals.", "Series: **R = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>**; parallel: **1/R = 1/R<sub>1</sub> + 1/R<sub>2</sub>**.", "**H = I<sup>2</sup>Rt; P = VI; 1 kWh = 3.6 x 10<sup>6</sup> J.**", "Ammeter in **series**, voltmeter in **parallel**; household wiring is parallel with a fuse."])
